  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Sunday/...\nThe long term forecast remains largely uneventful. Clearing skies\nwill continue take over the rest of the panhandle from north to \nsouth Wednesday. The low pressure system bringing rainfall to the \nsouthern panhandle Tuesday meanders southward through Wednesday, \ncontinuing a chance of scattered showers for parts of the southern\nand central panhandle through the afternoon. Shower potential \nwill diminish into Thursday as an area of ridging shifts eastward \nthrough the gulf, persisting through Saturday and bringing \nnorthwesterly flow, drier conditions, and warmer temperatures \nthrough the second half of the week. High temperatures will reach \nthe high 60s to low 70s in the northern panhandle and the low to \nmid 70s in the southern panhandle Thursday and Friday with ample \nclearing.\n\nNortherly winds will increase up to 25 mph through Lynn Canal and\ninto Northern Chatham Strait overnight from Tuesday into \nWednesday, decrease through the day, and increase again through \nThursday morning. Winds will decrease again overnight into Friday \nmorning before the flow switches southerly through the afternoon \nas the pressure gradient turns. Models are in high disagreement \ngoing into the weekend, with the EC holding onto the ridge in the \ngulf through the weekend and the GFS favoring an upper level \nshortwave making it over the ridge and into the northern panhandle\nSaturday. Depending on how things change over the next few days, \nwe could have a very dry or very wet weekend.",
